WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:03.359
Everyone wants clean water, and

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.159
America's public waterways haven't

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:06.160
always been very clean.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.209
In 1969, the Cuyahoga

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:11.369
River in Ohio was so polluted

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:12.899
by Cleveland's manufacturing

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.449
industry that it caught on fire.

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:16.979
Bigger fires had engulfed the river

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:18.869
before, but coinciding as it

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:20.369
did with the rise of the modern

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:21.569
environmental movement, the

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.669
spectacle inspired a Time magazine

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:25.559
feature describing a river

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:28.319
that "oozes rather than flows."

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.569
In 1970, Nixon

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.699
signed the National Environmental

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.589
Policy Act, and later

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:35.939
that year he established the

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:38.249
Environmental Protection Agency

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.199
to make a coordinated attack on

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.119
the pollutants which "debase the air

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:44.279
we breathe, the water we drink,

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.739
and the land that grows our food."

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:48.149
But in fighting to reduce the

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:50.429
pollution of air, land

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:52.409
and water, the EPA

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:54.269
has dictated to Americans what

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:56.309
they can do on their property,

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:57.689
even when it has no clear

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:00.029
environmental benefit or exceeds

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:01.859
the agency's authority.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:03.629
Now, the EPA's broad mandate for the

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:05.488
first time since its creation is

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:07.889
facing a serious constitutional

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:08.819
challenge.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:10.679
It's a case that started 15 years

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:12.689
ago with a couple living in a small

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:14.009
town in Idaho.

00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:15.929
When I was in high school,

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:18.029
I was up there camping.

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:19.349
And fell in love with Priest Lake,

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:20.339
and just had to try and figure out

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:21.269
how to live there.

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:22.649
There's no other place you'd want to

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:25.409
be. It is so peaceful and calm.

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:27.089
Mike and Chantelle Sackett purchased

00:01:27.090 --> 00:01:28.529
the tract of land abutting an

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:30.389
easement, which guaranteed them

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:33.089
a prime view of Priest Lake.

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:34.409
They plan to leverage their

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:36.389
background in construction to build

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:39.089
the lakefront home of their dreams.

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:41.009
A few days into construction,

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:42.599
the Sackett received a surprise

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:44.699
visit from the EPA and Army

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:45.869
Corps of Engineers.

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:47.759
They walked on to the property and

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:48.899
said, You need to stop work

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:50.699
immediately. He said, Why?

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:51.809
They said, Because we think you're

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:53.399
filling in wetlands.

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:55.169
The wetlands the government's agents

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:57.059
were referring to was a

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.279
residential lot in an established

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.529
subdivision with a full sewer hookup

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.539
100 yards from the lake and

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:05.789
a county title with no indication

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:07.259
of wetlands status.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:09.299
There was a nearby ditch draining

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:11.069
into a stream to connect to the

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:12.959
lake. It was separated from the

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:14.969
lot by 30 feet of paved

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:16.919
road, the proximity of the second

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:18.779
land to the ditch, in addition

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:21.059
to the existence of a subterranean

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:22.679
water flow discovered beneath their

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:23.680
lot as they began construction

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:26.789
meant that the residential lot was

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:29.129
a federally protected wetland,

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:30.659
according to the EPA.

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:32.099
Although the seconds faced up to

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:33.929
$75,000 a day for

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:35.549
violation of the Clean Water Act in

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:37.409
the compliance order, the EPA

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:38.639
argued they had no right to

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:40.469
challenge them in court until they

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:42.299
actually took action to impose

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.219
and collect the fine, which they

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:46.409
could do retroactively at any time.

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:48.539
With this threat looming over them,

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:49.540
the Sackett's paused construction.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:53.039
The EPA also wanted the seconds

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.764
to remove the gravel they had

00:02:54.765 --> 00:02:56.819
poured, fence in the lot, plant

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:59.758
foliage, but the couple refused.

00:02:59.759 --> 00:03:01.299
'We want you to fence it, and then

00:03:01.300 --> 00:03:02.999
we want you to plant these wetlands

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:04.169
plants.

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:05.999
And then we want you to watch it

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.829
for 3 to 5 years and

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:09.809
make notes. And and

00:03:09.810 --> 00:03:11.189
we'll be able to look at that.' And

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:12.419
I go, 'Are you kidding me?'

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:14.459
Why would we do that?

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:16.169
I mean, it's a lot in a subdivision.

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:17.819
Do you want to create a wetland?

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:20.969
That was in 2012.

00:03:20.970 --> 00:03:22.769
The Sacketts' case went all the way

00:03:22.770 --> 00:03:24.509
to the Supreme Court, which ruled

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:26.399
unanimously that the EPA's

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:28.109
compliance orders were indeed

00:03:28.110 --> 00:03:30.179
subject to judicial review,

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:31.439
meaning the agency couldn't

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:33.239
retroactively fine the circuits for

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:34.739
being in violation of the order.

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:35.849
As the court challenge was

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:37.169
adjudicated.

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:39.149
Ten years later, Supreme Court

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:40.919
is taking up the next part of that

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:42.839
case, a challenge to how

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:45.659
the agency defines a wetland.

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:47.519
The first decision got

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:49.199
us the right to get into court and

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:50.789
now we hope to finally secure that

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:51.689
victory.

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:53.279
Damien Schiff is an attorney with

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:55.169
the Pacific Legal Foundation, the

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:57.059
nonprofit law firm that is arguing

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:58.529
the case before the Supreme Court

00:03:58.530 --> 00:03:59.639
for a second time.

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:01.829
He says a favorable ruling could

00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:04.139
finally constrain a federal agency

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:06.989
that routinely bullies landowners.

00:04:06.990 --> 00:04:09.089
The big picture is a

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:11.039
dispute that's roiled the

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:12.509
property rights and environmental

00:04:12.510 --> 00:04:14.369
law communities for a half a decade,

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:16.469
and that is the scope of the Clean

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:18.148
Water Act.

00:04:18.149 --> 00:04:20.009
The EPA and the Army Corps, who are

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:21.599
the agencies that administer this

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.369
law, have over the last several

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:25.289
decades, used their regulatory

00:04:25.290 --> 00:04:27.209
authority to radically expand

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:29.189
what qualifies as water

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:30.389
in the United States.

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:32.369
The Clean Water Act allows the EPA

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:34.679
to regulate all of the country's

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:36.659
"navigable waters."

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:38.489
From rivers to lakes

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:41.429
to streams to oceanic channels.

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:43.019
But the definition of navigable

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:45.509
water has steadily expanded

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:48.599
since the passage in 1972.

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:50.669
Farmers like Curtis Martin weren't

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:52.469
allowed to add a manmade lake that

00:04:52.470 --> 00:04:53.999
increased the biodiversity of his

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:56.099
land because EPA said it violated

00:04:56.100 --> 00:04:57.449
the act.

00:04:57.450 --> 00:04:59.339
John Duarte almost lost

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:01.039
his farm in California's Central

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:02.959
Valley after EPA fined

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:04.969
him more than $30 million

00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:06.859
in restoration fees.

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:08.479
Reason coveraged Duarte's story.

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:10.309
Back in 2017.

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:11.689
These federal prosecutors can come

00:05:11.690 --> 00:05:13.609
in like the sheriff in Nottingham,

00:05:13.610 --> 00:05:14.929
decide for themselves what they

00:05:14.930 --> 00:05:17.089
think a family can pay, decide

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:18.019
for themselves if they want to

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:19.939
destroy a family and take their land

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:21.799
away. If the federal prosecutors can

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:23.719
come on this farm with this set

00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:25.459
of facts, there is no farm in

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:27.409
America that is safe from this kind

00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:29.029
of prosecution.

00:05:29.030 --> 00:05:30.799
The Supreme Court weighed in on the

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:32.809
EPA's expansive authority

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:35.059
to regulate land in a 2006

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:37.069
case in which the agency had tried

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:39.169
to stop a Michigan developer named

00:05:39.170 --> 00:05:41.029
John Rapanos from turning

00:05:41.030 --> 00:05:43.189
part of his 54-acre property

00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:45.229
into a shopping mall, even though

00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:46.909
it was more than 11 miles from the

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:48.889
nearest navigable water.

00:05:48.890 --> 00:05:50.659
Because the land became swampy in

00:05:50.660 --> 00:05:52.699
the spring, as the snow melted, they

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:55.009
argued that Rapanos' development

00:05:55.010 --> 00:05:57.379
plan would destroy protected

00:05:57.380 --> 00:05:58.459
wetlands.

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:00.589
He sued and a 5-4 majority

00:06:00.590 --> 00:06:03.079
vacated the ruling against Rapanos.

00:06:03.080 --> 00:06:05.119
But Justice Kennedy declined

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:07.459
to join Scalia's plurality opinion

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:08.989
that would have further limited the

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:11.749
agency's regulatory authority.

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:13.849
Scalia wrote that "The standards

00:06:13.850 --> 00:06:15.979
the government sought gave the EPA

00:06:15.980 --> 00:06:17.539
and Army Corps of Engineers

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:19.399
jurisdiction over between

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:21.599
270 to 300

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:23.509
million acres of swampy

00:06:23.510 --> 00:06:25.969
lands, including half of Alaska

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:28.159
and an area the size of California

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:30.139
in the lower 48 states,

00:06:30.140 --> 00:06:31.699
and that landowners spent more than

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:33.589
$1.7 billion a year

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:35.509
obtaining wetlands permits."

00:06:35.510 --> 00:06:37.669
Kennedy rejected Scalia's reasoning

00:06:37.670 --> 00:06:39.829
and devised the Significant

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:41.689
Nexis Standard, which

00:06:41.690 --> 00:06:43.159
gave the government the authority to

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:45.319
regulate the land if

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:47.239
the pollution would "significantly

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:49.579
affect the chemical, physical

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:51.469
and biological integrity of nearby

00:06:51.470 --> 00:06:53.419
navigable water." Kennedy's

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:55.099
standard carried the day in lower

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:56.989
courts, but Schiff says

00:06:56.990 --> 00:06:58.969
it's far too ambiguous to hold

00:06:58.970 --> 00:07:00.139
any longer.

00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:02.209
That ambiguity is one

00:07:02.210 --> 00:07:03.769
that, unfortunately, EPA and the

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:05.239
Corps have made a little too much

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:07.099
of. And I think that's really what

00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:09.139
this case is, is going

00:07:09.140 --> 00:07:11.059
we hope to do, is to

00:07:11.060 --> 00:07:12.889
to make it clear that

00:07:12.890 --> 00:07:14.749
a small ambiguity does not mean

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:16.789
carp blanche to regulate anything.

00:07:16.790 --> 00:07:18.109
We also recognize that it's

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:20.149
sometimes hard to tell going

00:07:20.150 --> 00:07:21.799
from the middle of a river or a lake

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:23.479
to the shore.

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:24.919
At what point are we still in the

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:26.179
lake or the river? And at what point

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:28.099
are we now on solid ground?

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:30.199
And that transition zone oftentimes

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:32.179
is occupied by things like wetlands.

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:33.889
But that, of course, is not what EPA

00:07:33.890 --> 00:07:35.329
and the Corps want to do.

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:36.619
They don't really see any need to

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:38.149
have any sort of boundary drawing

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:39.589
problem. They think wetlands on

00:07:39.590 --> 00:07:41.449
their own, even wetlands that are de

00:07:41.450 --> 00:07:43.279
facto isolated from

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:44.689
any other waters could still be

00:07:44.690 --> 00:07:45.949
regulated. That is, in fact,

00:07:45.950 --> 00:07:47.749
actually the case for this act.

00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:49.279
I mean, there are a lot is bounded

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:50.839
by other developed properties and

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:52.759
roads and there's no surface

00:07:52.760 --> 00:07:54.469
water connection from their lot to

00:07:54.470 --> 00:07:56.119
any other plausible water.

00:07:56.120 --> 00:07:57.619
So they are effectively isolated.

00:07:57.620 --> 00:07:59.569
Nevertheless, EPA and the Corps

00:07:59.570 --> 00:08:01.309
think that they can regulate their

00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:03.259
property as waters of the United

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:04.219
States. So that's the big

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:05.749
differences. Sure, there is some

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:07.819
ambiguity, but it's not nearly

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:09.739
as ambiguous as EPA and the

00:08:09.740 --> 00:08:11.269
Army Corps think.

00:08:11.270 --> 00:08:13.459
But the EPA argues that it needs

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:15.019
the power to regulate properties

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:16.819
like the Sacketts because human

00:08:16.820 --> 00:08:18.769
activity on nearby lands can have

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:20.569
detrimental effect on protected

00:08:20.570 --> 00:08:22.429
waters like Priest Lake.

00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:24.019
They point to the Clean Water Act's

00:08:24.020 --> 00:08:26.179
authorization to regulate land

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:28.039
"adjacent to protected

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:29.959
water." But how far

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:32.298
does adjacency extend?

00:08:32.299 --> 00:08:34.099
That question was raised by Justice

00:08:34.100 --> 00:08:36.979
Neil Gorsuch in oral arguments.

00:08:36.980 --> 00:08:38.239
Despite the fact that there's a

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:40.069
subdivision between this

00:08:40.070 --> 00:08:41.928
property and the lake, it's still

00:08:41.929 --> 00:08:43.609
adjacent to the lake.

00:08:43.610 --> 00:08:44.509
That's the government's view of

00:08:44.510 --> 00:08:44.809
that.

00:08:44.810 --> 00:08:46.129
And it's adjacent.

00:08:46.130 --> 00:08:47.989
Why? What's the definition of

00:08:47.990 --> 00:08:49.549
adjacency?

00:08:49.550 --> 00:08:50.599
I think we are talking about

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:51.859
adjacency, and that may not be

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:53.179
something that gives you bright line

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:54.649
rules, but it rules out things that

00:08:54.650 --> 00:08:55.969
are many miles away.

00:08:55.970 --> 00:08:57.909
Sure, the EPA would take that view.

00:08:57.910 --> 00:08:59.719
The agencies have told me they do

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:01.159
not draw bright line rules.

00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:02.719
They do not think 300 feet is

00:09:02.720 --> 00:09:04.009
unreasonable for adjacency.

00:09:04.010 --> 00:09:06.229
So how about 3,000 feet?

00:09:06.230 --> 00:09:07.009
Could be.

00:09:07.010 --> 00:09:08.301
I don't know the answer to that.

00:09:08.302 --> 00:09:09.799
Could it be three miles?

00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:10.949
I don't think it could be.

00:09:10.950 --> 00:09:12.359
Could it be two miles?

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:13.759
Then again, when we start to talk

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:15.679
about miles, that sounds too

00:09:15.680 --> 00:09:16.699
far to the to...

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:17.659
One mile?

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:18.559
To me again.

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:19.759
And I see where this is headed.

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:20.760
But but again, I think

00:09:22.070 --> 00:09:22.488
so.

00:09:22.489 --> 00:09:23.689
So if the federal government doesn't

00:09:23.690 --> 00:09:25.819
know, how is a person

00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:27.709
subject to criminal time

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:29.689
in federal prison supposed

00:09:29.690 --> 00:09:30.649
to know?

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:32.569
So the agency is in recognition

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:34.159
of this problem, make available free

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:35.329
of charge jurisdictional

00:09:35.330 --> 00:09:37.879
determinations as to any property.

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:39.769
They also publicize their manuals

00:09:39.770 --> 00:09:41.092
and make available on websites...

00:09:42.109 --> 00:09:42.949
Their manuals that don't tell us the

00:09:42.950 --> 00:09:44.119
answer.

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:45.949
There's no doubt that an important

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:48.139
part of water quality is wetlands

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:49.849
regulation. I mean, nobody denies

00:09:49.850 --> 00:09:50.629
that.

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:52.249
But the question is, is that

00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:54.649
something that Congress intended

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:56.659
to implement at the federal level

00:09:56.660 --> 00:09:58.249
in the Clean Water Act now?

00:09:58.250 --> 00:09:59.889
The. Best way to figure that out is,

00:09:59.890 --> 00:10:01.329
well, let's look at the text.

00:10:01.330 --> 00:10:03.159
The text did it mention wetlands?

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:04.839
If the ruling goes this way, there's

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:06.759
going to be a lot of people

00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:08.679
that are afraid that now

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:10.989
polluters are just unshackled to

00:10:10.990 --> 00:10:13.419
dump stuff into bodies of water

00:10:13.420 --> 00:10:15.879
or, you know, nearby wetlands

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:17.679
that could affect bodies of water.

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:19.659
And we're all going to be worse off

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:20.679
for it.

00:10:20.680 --> 00:10:21.999
What would you say to somebody who

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:23.019
has that concern?

00:10:23.020 --> 00:10:24.459
Just because the feds don't regulate

00:10:24.460 --> 00:10:25.929
doesn't mean that state and local

00:10:25.930 --> 00:10:27.279
governments can't regulate.

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:28.779
And in fact, there are examples of

00:10:28.780 --> 00:10:30.819
where when the Supreme Court

00:10:30.820 --> 00:10:32.499
has limited the scope of the Clean

00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:34.239
Water Act in prior cases, some

00:10:34.240 --> 00:10:36.279
states decided afterwards, 'okay,

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:38.049
we're going to expand our own state

00:10:38.050 --> 00:10:39.879
water quality regulations to

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:41.799
capture those areas that are

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:43.599
now not federally regulated.' Other

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:45.129
states have chosen different paths,

00:10:45.130 --> 00:10:47.469
but it's certainly possible for that

00:10:47.470 --> 00:10:49.539
to be a supplement

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:51.459
for areas where people think

00:10:51.460 --> 00:10:52.839
federal regulation is needed that

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:54.699
may no longer be provided.

00:10:54.700 --> 00:10:56.649
Images of burning rivers

00:10:56.650 --> 00:10:58.539
can serve as dramatic reminders

00:10:58.540 --> 00:11:00.069
of the costs of uncontrolled

00:11:00.070 --> 00:11:01.059
pollution.

00:11:01.060 --> 00:11:02.319
But that doesn't mean that the

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:03.759
federal government is the best

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:06.489
entity to protect natural resources.

00:11:06.490 --> 00:11:08.169
In Florida, the Army Corps of

00:11:08.170 --> 00:11:10.629
Engineers has for years overridden

00:11:10.630 --> 00:11:12.549
local authorities to divert sludge

00:11:12.550 --> 00:11:14.499
from the state's largest lake into

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:16.329
lagoons and estuaries,

00:11:16.330 --> 00:11:18.459
introducing toxic algae blooms

00:11:18.460 --> 00:11:19.959
that have wreaked havoc on the local

00:11:19.960 --> 00:11:22.359
ecosystem and introduced serious

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:23.649
health hazards.

00:11:23.650 --> 00:11:25.659
Ironically, in possible violation

00:11:25.660 --> 00:11:27.489
of the Clean Water Act it's supposed

00:11:27.490 --> 00:11:28.809
to help enforce.

00:11:28.810 --> 00:11:30.639
As the Army Corps of Engineers

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:32.469
transferred toxic water

00:11:32.470 --> 00:11:34.869
toxic water from Lake Okeechobee

00:11:34.870 --> 00:11:36.729
to the east through the C434

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:38.139
Reservoir into the Saint Lucia

00:11:38.140 --> 00:11:40.179
estuary and the Indian River Lagoon,

00:11:40.180 --> 00:11:41.079
and to the west through the

00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:42.279
Caloosahatchee River.

00:11:42.280 --> 00:11:44.649
Yes, sir. We have we have

00:11:44.650 --> 00:11:46.749
conveyed water out of the system

00:11:46.750 --> 00:11:48.879
that has contained cyanobacteria

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:50.289
and harmful algae blooms.

00:11:50.290 --> 00:11:50.979
Yes, sir.

00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:53.739
And the Corps considers that toxic.

00:11:53.740 --> 00:11:54.669
Yes, sir.

00:11:54.670 --> 00:11:56.259
It's hard to regulate wetlands

00:11:56.260 --> 00:11:57.999
federally and still preserve

00:11:58.000 --> 00:11:59.229
traditional state and local

00:11:59.230 --> 00:12:00.909
authority over land use.

00:12:00.910 --> 00:12:02.799
A big, broad federal law

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:04.749
is oftentimes not the best way to

00:12:04.750 --> 00:12:06.729
resolve environmental issues,

00:12:06.730 --> 00:12:08.739
not just because it crowds out

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:10.209
state and local efforts, but perhaps

00:12:10.210 --> 00:12:11.679
more importantly, it crowds out

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:13.329
private party efforts.

00:12:13.330 --> 00:12:14.769
When you have the federal government

00:12:14.770 --> 00:12:17.019
threatening significant fines

00:12:17.020 --> 00:12:18.489
for any sort of activity that may

00:12:18.490 --> 00:12:20.319
affect waters and also

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:21.849
making it much more difficult to do

00:12:21.850 --> 00:12:23.739
anything in terms of private

00:12:23.740 --> 00:12:24.819
conservation.

00:12:24.820 --> 00:12:26.379
Schiff says he expects a ruling in

00:12:26.380 --> 00:12:27.999
early 2023.

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:29.859
The more than a decade long ordeal

00:12:29.860 --> 00:12:32.229
has taken its toll on the Sacketts

00:12:32.230 --> 00:12:33.699
who never completed work on the

00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:34.509
home.

00:12:34.510 --> 00:12:35.919
But if their case prevails for a

00:12:35.920 --> 00:12:38.049
second time in the Supreme Court,

00:12:38.050 --> 00:12:39.519
they'll not only have established

00:12:39.520 --> 00:12:41.139
the clear right for citizens to

00:12:41.140 --> 00:12:42.729
challenge powerful executive

00:12:42.730 --> 00:12:45.069
agencies like the EPA in court,

00:12:45.070 --> 00:12:46.869
but also the rights of property

00:12:46.870 --> 00:12:49.059
owners to improve their lands

00:12:49.060 --> 00:12:51.339
without exorbitant compliance costs

00:12:51.340 --> 00:12:53.169
and legal threats from the federal

00:12:53.170 --> 00:12:53.769
government.

00:12:53.770 --> 00:12:55.689
Most private property rights

00:12:55.690 --> 00:12:57.909
violations nowadays happen

00:12:57.910 --> 00:13:00.069
because of environmentally motivated

00:13:00.070 --> 00:13:01.749
laws or environmentally motivated

00:13:01.750 --> 00:13:02.979
lawsuits.

00:13:02.980 --> 00:13:04.959
And to fight back against that,

00:13:04.960 --> 00:13:06.159
you have to go straight to the

00:13:06.160 --> 00:13:07.269
statutes themselves.

00:13:07.270 --> 00:13:09.399
So a win here to

00:13:09.400 --> 00:13:11.259
appropriately narrow

00:13:11.260 --> 00:13:12.969
the scope of EPA's in the court's

00:13:12.970 --> 00:13:14.409
authority under the Clean Water Act

00:13:14.410 --> 00:13:16.119
would be a great achievement for our

00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:17.320
multi-decade efforts.

